	<body>
		<outline text="October 2026" created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 17:47:22 GMT" name="october2026" type="calendarMonth">
			<outline text="October 31" created="Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:47:05 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="31">
				<outline text="The soup the whole soup and nothing but the soup so help me soup." created="Sat, 31 Oct 2026 17:42:19 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Feature request for twitter. I'd like to have a page listing all my tweets today on one page, so I can link to them from my blog. I would like to see twitter go back to its leading role in support of the web." created="Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:50:15 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Two new attributes added to the &lt;a href=&quot;https://source.scripting.com/#1716309198000&quot;&gt;source:inReplyTo element&lt;/a&gt; in RSS.chat feeds, feedUrl and feedTitle. These were requested by developers, and they seemed useful and reasonable, so we added them. Still more work to do here in creating a world wide discourse system, not one based on a single central node. RSS has more work to do for us. ;-)" created="Sat, 31 Oct 2026 16:42:14 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="BTW, I have been writing &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner&quot;&gt;on Twitter&lt;/a&gt; the way I used to write on my blog. Paragraph after paragraph, and I go on, but I do eventually re-read it and it has the energy I haven't had in a long time. I think people now realize we are stuck. And in networking systems, I'm the guy you go to when you want to get unstuck. Reminds me of that great speech in &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/NYknJmoDDPs?si=q1sRZQfP_0ifCz6a&amp;t=24&quot;&gt;Michael Clayton&lt;/a&gt;: &quot;I'm not the guy you kill. I'm the guy you buy! Are you so fucking blind that you don't even see what I am? I'm the easiest part of your problem, and you're gonna kill me? You're so fucked.&quot; " created="Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:47:06 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2019/09/08/theTruthCanBeAdjusted.png"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 30" created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:06:13 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="30">
				<outline text="I've been watching short videos about training dogs, and really opening my eyes, and it helps when working with Claude. Always try to be positive, until there is behaviour you have to correct and then yank the leash, and when they do the right thing reward them with a treat (or whatever passes as a treat)." created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 20:29:37 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Highly recommend the &lt;a href=&quot;https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/heather-cox-richardson-on-why-the-republican-party-no/id1498802610?i=1000798669132&quot;&gt;interview&lt;/a&gt; between Scott Galloway and Heather Cox Richardson. Quite a combination of minds. Don't skip to the end but don't miss it, they explain how blogging allowed them to route around the gatekeepers. That's the point. But imho you have to leave the door open to those who are inspired by you, and Substack isn't the best place for them. Please keep your ears open to new less tech industry ways of getting your ideas out. The bigco's haven't just screwed up the political system, they've also screwed up the web. The web needs all our help as does democracy, and the two imho are closely related." created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 19:54:37 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/issues/365&quot;&gt;Braintrust query&lt;/a&gt;: Does Google define the web?" created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 15:14:04 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="New &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/30/smallPiecespng.png&quot;&gt;header graphic&lt;/a&gt; for scripting.com. Did this one with ChatGPT, asked it to make a &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Burma-Shave&quot;&gt;Burma-Shave&lt;/a&gt; sign sequence and gave it a few examples, and now it knows how to do another one of these for me in the future. I've been using it that way to come up with desktop icons for my apps. " created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 16:02:34 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Yesterday I &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/29/143258.html&quot;&gt;called&lt;/a&gt; the Knicks championship the &quot;greatest sports win in a long time,&quot; but I have to say the fans of the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2026/oct/29/milwaukee-brewers-world-series-title-mlb-salary-cap&quot;&gt;Milwaukee Brewers&lt;/a&gt; would have cause to object. They had an incredible &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Milwaukee_Brewers_season&quot;&gt;season&lt;/a&gt; in MLB, were written off by the pundits much the same way they wrote off last season's Knicks. They even won the World Series in the same &lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=%22knicks%20in%205%22&quot;&gt;Knicks in 5&lt;/a&gt; way the Knicks did it, with a sweep of the much-hated Dodgers, with their better pedigree along the way. So to my &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wisconsin&quot;&gt;Wiscowtown&lt;/a&gt; friends, who are holding their parade today, congrats on your first championship in 53 years like the Knicks, or more accurately their first championship &lt;i&gt;ever. &lt;/i&gt;" created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:14:23 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/30/fuckemBucky.png"/>
				<outline text="Yesteday I sent a snapshot of the scripting.com bucket from AWS to Claude and asked it to analyze it, what's in it, what's taking up all the space, and got back an excellent report. Overnight I thought of another question, summarized in this prompt. &quot;this is fascinating. i wonder if we could reconstruct my blog from before may 2017 from the source materials in this folder? i am converting to markdown in the bonbon project and i think we'll find a lot of the scripting.com content here, and i believe much of it will be convertible. also, over the years i tried blogging experiments on different domains, but i imagine there are links here to those sites. maybe if we did a research project here we could make one site out of this writing, and give it a markdown treatment so bonbon can do the presentation?&quot; Now one thing to know is that Claude never says it can't do something, but the plan I got was inspiring. Now I have more to think about and will connect the two projects so they can compare notes, although the scripting.com project already checked out bonbon, it's amazing how quickly it can absorb a whole codebase and all the planning that went into it." created="Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:06:14 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 29" created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 13:58:51 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="29">
				<outline text="People in ChatGPT-land are &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/idbeatty/status/2115767635514114183&quot;&gt;talking about Frontier&lt;/a&gt;. Not sure who they are or what the context is, but I agree, and want to do more integrations between our codebase and the AI runtimes. " created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 19:13:00 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2115790799367553521&quot;&gt;Posted on Twitter&lt;/a&gt; re Frontier being real in 2026, not just a memory.  " created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:42:17 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Knicks settle down" created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:32:58 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="I was pretty sure the Knicks just needed to settle down, but the fans and the city and journalists and to some extent the team were still living mentally in June, in the aftermath of the greatest sports win in a long time. Then the team lost three, probably because they too were in the bubble of last year. This is the team that when asked how they felt about their chances in the playoffs when they were sweeping a team like the Sixers or Cavs, they would say -- the score in the next game is 0-0.  If you believe it was your shit that made you win, try again. It's why rich people lead such miserable lives. They think they won the game, heh but no one wins the game, you may just get a reprieve to play again in the next season, as the Knicks are doing now too." created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:33:11 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Last night's game was a perfect demo of why we love this version of the team so much. When someone isn't making their shots, they sub a bench player who adds the spark they needed, and everyone plays with gusto. There's always a new man up on this team or so it seems." created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:37:37 GMT"/>
					<outline text="It was good that everyone got the doom and gloom out of their systems, don't mistake this season as a repeat of last season. You're not entitled to anything and bet against it being quite different. Everyone is a year older. Mitchell Robinson is gone. But last night we were reminded of the overall character of the team which seems now to be constant. Every freaking bench player is a phenomenon. How did they get such a bench? Great business people who understand the sport, and coaching that knows how to manage a talented team to go all the way, and players that are close to each other, and feel the team. " created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:36:42 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I said this durning the doom and gloom, I could cruise for another 50 years  on the high from last season. But I am ready for more love from this team and from the city that loves the Knicks even when they suck (please remember that) and we should feel no less proud when they don't." created="Thu, 29 Oct 2026 14:40:39 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 28" created="Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:36:05 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="28">
				<outline text="I'm doing a little project on rss.chat today and dusting off the cobwebs of my brain. Since I last worked on this project, I have converted Frontier to run on modern OSes and written a whole new app. Only three months, but I have no recollection, even reading the code isn't helping much. This is when it's incredibly good to have Claude Code on the job, I can ask it to guide me through the process we worked out which feels like it was 15 years ago instead of 15 weeks. It has complete recall. And even though it didn't work on Frontier, that was a different Claude project, it was able to debug something that broke in the build script in the interim. I've spent the whole year teaching Claude how to be me. Is that a source of immortality? I won't be around to figure that part out. " created="Wed, 28 Oct 2026 15:30:19 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/28/dave.png"/>
				<outline text="Braintrust query: &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/issues/364&quot;&gt;Why do you love Markdown?&lt;/a&gt; I have a theory why people love it so much -- simplicity. You can't hide any garbage in a Markdown file. You can answer on &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/issues/364&quot;&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2115445171768717495&quot;&gt;Twitter&lt;/a&gt;. Didn't post on Masto or Bluesky because the prompt is 761 chars, over the limit for both systems. I think I'm finally done with truncating well-written prose to fit into one vendor's idea of what's enough text for me. I want to make that a distant memory.  So another thing I love about Markdown -- no freaking character limit. " created="Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:03:20 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I really like the Apple TV show &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Small_Prophets&quot;&gt;Small Prophets&lt;/a&gt;. It's silly and fun, but also intelligent and beautifully presented. &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Palin&quot;&gt;Michael Palin&lt;/a&gt; of Monty Python is a supporting actor playing the main character's father.  He's famous for a lot of Monty Python sketches, esp the one about the dead parrot. &quot;He's not dead, he's pining for the fjords.&quot;" created="Wed, 28 Oct 2026 13:36:06 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 27" created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:13:41 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="27">
				<outline text="Yesterday Ben Thompson &lt;a href=&quot;https://stratechery.com/2026/the-end-of-the-internet/&quot;&gt;wrote&lt;/a&gt; that we've reached the end of the web, and I had come to the same conclusion, just a few days ago. And his piece, which was a great milestone, said it's not totally gone, because of the advent of AI we can build small systems for each other. Which is exactly the motivation of the &lt;a href=&quot;https://bigundo.org/&quot;&gt;BigUndo&lt;/a&gt; model. What's big about it is how small the parts are and how nicely they fit together. We're not leaving everything behind in the great diaspora to the new web, we're not just bringing Markdown with us. It also echoes &lt;a href=&quot;https://marco.org/2026/10/24/community&quot;&gt;Marco Arment&lt;/a&gt;, who wrote a similar piece about Mastodon a few days before. Nothing wrong with Mastodon, but the idea of one model of service doing the work for everyone is ridiculous. We need a million different approaches, all we need to make it work is a standard they can all agree on for what forms of text move around the net. Once we agree on a format, we all can use whatever editor we like, and no more &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2024/01/14/031201.html&quot;&gt;tiny little text boxes&lt;/a&gt; that lock your ideas into Substack or X or whatever. That was the crime, that's what the BigUndo is all about undoing. The web said very clearly what text is, and they all broke it by doing it differently. We're bringing RSS 2.0 and Markdown with us, and they were &lt;a href=&quot;https://source.scripting.com/markdown.opml&quot;&gt;joined&lt;/a&gt; a short time ago. Almost no one noticed, but there was design here, an intent -- a hope that we'd get to the place it appears we are now at. " created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:23:41 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/27/nellyBly.png"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zettelkasten&quot;&gt;Zettelkasten&lt;/a&gt; is an idea &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2023/01/24/150122.html&quot;&gt;pioneered&lt;/a&gt; by my great-uncle &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arno_Schmidt&quot;&gt;Arno Schmidt&lt;/a&gt;. It's a process for organizing your data and thoughts. When I read people's descriptions of what they do with it, it sounds totally like the outliners I pioneered two generations later. I have a Zettlekasten &lt;a href=&quot;https://zettelkasten.de/posts/note-writing-deliberate-mind-training/&quot;&gt;blog&lt;/a&gt; in my blogroll, &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/27/zettleKasten.png&quot;&gt;screenshot&lt;/a&gt;, so I think of &lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=%22arno%20schmidt%22&quot;&gt;Uncle Arno&lt;/a&gt;, who I never met, from time to time. " created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:18:56 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="If Claude Code projects had feelings, I'd wake up the Frontier project and let it know that the reason it's been asleep so long is that the software is working. I'm using it to manage the other three apps, writing code, keeping lists, everything works. Yes, the menubar is very out of date, but I know where to find everything. I look forward to coming in for a landing at some point and upgrade it work among the different BigUndo stuff we have running, no doubt we're going to use Frontier to do cross-app scripting. Some of the things AI says they need to do, Frontier can do with the resources of an early 90s Macintosh. No big data centers. Keep that in mind. " created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 14:13:42 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 26" created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:23:31 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="26">
				<outline text="I loved the term &lt;a href=&quot;https://bigundo.org/&quot;&gt;Big Undo&lt;/a&gt; so much, of course I had to get the domain. I started putting up my usual placeholder site but realized I should do this page in Markdown, not HTML. It looks fabulous. Markdown is right. It's sobriety, go back to the beginning and put the potency in the ideas, give writers the space to write. Get the F out of my way with your freaky character limits. Anyway I'm full of manifesto these days. The money people strangled the web. I think it's time for the creative people to make it work for real." created="Tue, 27 Oct 2026 00:33:27 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I've never had a web browser that worked remotely like I wanted it to. But that might change soon. Big things are possible now. I have a bunch of projects that I'm working on with Claude and going back and forth between them. And they're even starting to communicate with each other, at my behest of course. It's an interesting time for people to realize that the web was much more powerful before all the big tech companies had their way with it. It makes about as much sense as the various big plans they all came up with that made the web necessary in the first place." created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 15:14:33 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="The Big Undo" created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:37:52 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="I remember how revolutionary the first web was, but then a series of tech companies took it over and “had their way” with it. " created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:38:44 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The first was Netscape and they added JavaScript to steal the thunder from “Sun” (a long story). "/>
					<outline text="Then Microsoft crushed Netscape by cutting off their air supply, and they piled up all kinds of nasty insecure features with predictable results, they made the web a paradise of viruses, making it largely unusable until Firefox replaced Microsoft, and their web was nicer, and smaller and they left out the viruses.  "/>
					<outline text="Google came along and quickly replaced it with Chrome and all was good for a while, until they decided it was just another corporate platform like Android and threw out all the early sites, so boring, they couldn’t make billions of dollars, and now what used to be the web is all but gone." created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:39:41 GMT"/>
					<outline text="What’s next? It’s time for The Big Undo. 😀" created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:40:19 GMT"/>
					<outline text="&lt;i&gt;The Big Undo&lt;/i&gt; came to me while I was writing my new profile on Twitter. It immediately reminded me of three things. " created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:40:51 GMT" flNumberedSubs="true">
						<outline text="Karl Anthony Towns, Knicks all-star and world champion center, is called Big Bodega. " created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:41:01 GMT"/>
						<outline text="It sounds like a title of a &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_Lewis&quot;&gt;Michael Lewis&lt;/a&gt; book, like The Big Short." created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:41:01 GMT"/>
						<outline text="And for some reason Queen Latifah singing &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y-Fz-_lkWzM&quot;&gt;When You're Good to Mama&lt;/a&gt; in Chicago.  " created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:43:21 GMT"/>
						</outline>
					<outline text="And of course I bought the &lt;a href=&quot;https://bigundo.org/&quot;&gt;domain&lt;/a&gt;. " created="Mon, 26 Oct 2026 23:38:50 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 25" created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:23:52 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="25">
				<outline text="Changed &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner&quot;&gt;my bio&lt;/a&gt; on Twitter: &quot;I make software to interop. Working on a new web designed for writers and readers and in place of HTML we use Markdown. Keep it super simple. The big undo.&quot;" created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:39:24 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Obama is doing the old Obama campaign schtick and it's something to see. He's got a few gray hairs, but he's still got the energy and chutzpah. I'd love to see him and Bernie Sanders on the same stage before this campaign is over. My favorite &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QclN6UnicMg&quot;&gt;Obama clip&lt;/a&gt; is him wiping the schmutz off his shoulder, I forget who he was putting down, but wow it so concise. I also really liked the time when &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=IHJeptnpNWU&quot;&gt;HRC flirted&lt;/a&gt; with him about her likeability. &quot;You're likeable enough,&quot; he said. Trump gets the votes not necessarily because people are what people call them, he is a comedian, I say this so many times, that's the charm. Young folk may not remember Don Rickles and Joan Rivers, but that's Trump, and the other two probably had a sweet side, but I don't think that's ever been said about Trump. I could be wrong. There's also a bit of George Carlin in there. " created="Sun, 25 Oct 2026 16:23:53 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 24" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:57:16 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="24">
				<outline text="Why did the Knicks lose the first two games of the season? It's a veteran team, they know how to start an NBA season, but the fans and the press aren't ready to move on yet. They're still reveling in the feeling of the Knicks winning. But imho there is no feeling with that, the feeling was in the moment, and man they did give us some moment. We will &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.instagram.com/reels/DZjTf0CEfN2/&quot;&gt;relish&lt;/a&gt; the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rpyJjecJnuI&quot;&gt;video&lt;/a&gt; for the rest of time. But for now, every game they play will feel significant because of who they &lt;i&gt;were&lt;/i&gt; not who they are now." created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:22:48 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2019/07/02/knicks.png"/>
				<outline text="Andrew Shell, who created and runs the rssCloud server we use here, is &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/rsscloud/socketsub/blob/main/README.md&quot;&gt;creating a connection&lt;/a&gt; from rssCloud and webSub to WebSockets. I've wanted this for a long time, but now I'm planning on a desktop version of FeedLand, we will need it, pretty soon for instant updates of feeds we are subscribed to. " created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:16:28 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="One of the reasons I'm working on the &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/23/151945.html?title=webOfMarkdown&quot;&gt;Web of Markdown&lt;/a&gt; is that my main blog of 32+ years is being kicked off the Google Web. I need a new home where the &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/googleAndHttp/&quot;&gt;web religion&lt;/a&gt; is still practiced, and one of the commandments of our religion is &quot;Google is a guest on the web, as we all are. Guests don't make the rules.&quot;" created="Sat, 24 Oct 2026 14:57:17 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 23" created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 14:54:48 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="23">
				<outline text="A blog &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/examples/scriptingInMarkdown/archive/2017/06/03/141214.md&quot;&gt;post&lt;/a&gt; from Scripting, in Markdown." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:20:06 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="The web of Markdown, part 3" created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:19:45 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="Previous posts on this topic: &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/06/005559.html?title=webOfMarkdown&quot;&gt;Oct 6&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/08/135551.html&quot;&gt;Oct 8&lt;/a&gt;." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:04:31 GMT"/>
					<outline text="What's new? I've got a browser running, and have been using it for a few weeks. We're not releasing it today because first I want people to think about the format we're using to represent a story, and get feedback, and see if other people can build on the same basis. " created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:06:29 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Three things to look at today." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:10:46 GMT" flBulletedSubs="true">
						<outline text="The &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/readme.md&quot;&gt;top level&lt;/a&gt; picture. " created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:10:51 GMT"/>
						<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/blob/main/examples/readme.md&quot;&gt;Nine examples&lt;/a&gt;." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:11:01 GMT"/>
						<outline text="Download the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/raw/refs/heads/main/examples/scriptingInMarkdown/archive.zip&quot;&gt;archive&lt;/a&gt;. (Chrome will complain about this link not being &quot;secure,&quot; but the archive comes from GitHub, so it's plenty secure -- they must be on a campaign to give sites like mine a black eye. I wouldn't put a link to a download that wasn't from a reputable site. People who read my blog know that, why doesn't Google with all their fancy AI servers? Feh.)" created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:11:06 GMT"/>
						</outline>
					<outline text="And if you have questions or comments." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:11:17 GMT" flBulletedSubs="true">
						<outline text="Use the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/markdownWeb/issues/1&quot;&gt;Issues section&lt;/a&gt; in the repo." created="Fri, 23 Oct 2026 15:11:23 GMT"/>
						</outline>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 22" created="Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:58:19 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="22">
				<outline text="I hope to see the &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/08/135551.html&quot;&gt;The Web of Markdown&lt;/a&gt; idea take the next step later today or early tomorrow at the latest. &lt;i&gt;Still diggin!&lt;/i&gt; " created="Thu, 22 Oct 2026 16:00:31 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Ooops, didn't get it done today. Please come back tomorrow. :-)" created="Thu, 22 Oct 2026 23:58:19 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 21" created="Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:44:31 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="21">
				<outline text="Perfect autumn day in the mountains spent mostly away from the computer. Still, some interesting new stuff to show tomorrow. " created="Wed, 21 Oct 2026 23:44:32 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 20" created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:12:40 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="20">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SqRvJLH_-vU&quot;&gt;Today's song&lt;/a&gt;, for the Knicks.  &quot;And when the morning light comes streaming in I get up and do it again.&quot; &lt;i&gt;Knicks in 5! &lt;/i&gt;" created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:47:22 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="My friend &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.goodreads.com/quotes/391717-steve-jobs-has-a-saying-that-a-players-hire-a&quot;&gt;Guy Kawasaki&lt;/a&gt; has a quote from the early Mac division at Apple. &quot;Steve Jobs has a saying that A players hire A players. But B players hire C players, and C players hire D players. It doesn't take long to get to F players. This trickle-down effect causes bozo explosions in companies.&quot; You see this over and over among the very rich in tech. The money is supposed to make them invincible, but they don't hire the best people, I guess, for fear of being found out to not be the genius that the money says they are.  " created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:12:41 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/20/guyKawasakiHeadShot.png"/>
				<outline text="I have a longtime friend from tech who is a San Antonio Spurs fan. I am from New York, and thus am a lifelong Knicks fan. Until tonight's game starts at the Garden between the Knicks and the Sixers with their new acquisitions from Boston and the Lakers, share the hopes of many other Atlantic Division teams who are gunning for the champion Knicks. But after the first jump ball in tonight's game the score is zero-zero, everyone is in first place and everyone is in last place. No one expected the Knicks to win, not even a die-hard fan like myself who often wore a &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/01/20/170415.html&quot;&gt;paper bag&lt;/a&gt; on his head to avoid having to defend the bozosity of the hometown team. They had some truly awful teams since 1999, last time they were in the finals. So what happened to the Knicks? How did they do it? See the quote from Guy Kawaski above. We hired an A team guy in &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leon_Rose&quot;&gt;Leon Rose&lt;/a&gt;, and he used his great insight into the sport of NBA basketball to assemble a team of second round but A team players who had the special magic that when you put them together on court, they blossomed into the most improbable winners ever. They were good, but no one but the players and the management knew how good they could be. Anyway I empathized with my Spurs fan friend, a very unusual thing for a NY sports fan, we prefer to find annoying ways of making fun of the fans of teams we have &lt;a href=&quot;https://dictionary.cambridge.org/us/dictionary/english/vanquished&quot;&gt;vanquished&lt;/a&gt;, but just this one time, well until the jump ball tonight, then Knicks own the NBA, then they're just another team who have to prove themselves all over again, from scratch. And we wouldn't have it any other way. &lt;i&gt;Knicks in 5!&lt;/i&gt;" created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:26:02 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="We do know what's going on" created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:51:14 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=%22occam%27s%20news%22&quot;&gt;Occam's News&lt;/a&gt; reports that Trump et al are selling out the US. " created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:51:21 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Putin can get the US to do what he wants by giving Trump money. Total win-win. And the US is flushed down the toilet. " created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 21:38:22 GMT"/>
					<outline text="That's all that's going on there. "/>
					<outline text="All of the feigned bafflement is make-believe. It's totally obvious what's going on. And don't forget Musk has the world encircled with satellites. If he doesn't want you communicating, you don't communicate. "/>
					<outline text="In other words the selling-out has been going on for some time." created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 12:51:59 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 19" created="Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:51:43 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="19">
				<outline text="Anyway -- I figured out what I want to do. I want to go back to the point where CSS and JavaScript were added to the web. Everything is a document. It's a &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2025/03/13/132830.html&quot;&gt;writer's web&lt;/a&gt;. The reader owns the look of the pages. The writer has no say in colors, or arrangements, it's just writing. No character limits. Simple styling. Full fidelity &lt;a href=&quot;https://textcasting.org/&quot;&gt;text&lt;/a&gt;. Reading and writing, and then the developers can come up with a million different ways to read, and everyone gets to use their favorite editor, always, and store all their writing where they live, and let everyone read it where ever they read. Any structure they can think of, but all the rendering code is with the reader. I think we can take what we've learned since the web got corporatized, and bring it back to the basic platform we had then. And btw, no HTML, just Markdown. :-)" created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:45:39 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/05/10/andNowForSomethingCompletelyDifferent.png"/>
				<outline text="I would like to have one day when Claude Code doesn't fuck everything up by doing things it is explicitly prohibited from doing. I find things that were changed in critical parts of the program, that were indefensible. One of the things we're doing is trying to establish a language for frontmatter for Markdown files that are meant to be displayed by a browser. It just invented one, gave it wrong default. I have to find these things on my own. And what happens when it wipes out a site without checking with me first. It has done this, this year. " created="Mon, 19 Oct 2026 13:51:44 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Postscript: Of course the blame for the wasted time this morning goes to me. Claude tries to do what it thought I wanted. If I calm down, figure out what the confusion really means, and reconsider the path I chose and pull back, we get much further. And I think the way I was trying to go was a mistake. I think film editing is a lot like this. Anyway we got past the problem, and today was a very productive day in the end. " created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:38:52 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="About &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/XSLT&quot;&gt;XSLT&lt;/a&gt; -- I remembered that I &lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=xslt&quot;&gt;thought&lt;/a&gt; it was a bad idea. Let XML look like XML. Trying to make it look like an HTML page, how does that make any sense. Like opening the hood of a car and seeing a big picture of the outside of the car. People aren't really that dumb, but some people like to complain a lot. The rule of the web is if you don't like what you see, that's what the freaking back button is there for. Probably just a troll anyway. Show them your JSON and RSS. If it's done nicely it can be quite illumifying. " created="Tue, 20 Oct 2026 00:55:02 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 18" created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:55:31 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="18">
				<outline text="I wrote a piece entitled &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/06/005559.html&quot;&gt;The Web of Markdown&lt;/a&gt; on Oct 6. I have to keep looking it up, but I do remember writing it very quickly and deciding it needed a memorable title, like the ones about textcasting, and podcasting and all the other bits that went on to be something. It's one of those times when it seems to have potential with other developers, they seem to see the same logic in it that I do. A way to disconnect from VC control of the web and try again with the open basis, the un-owned basis. There was a fork here at some point in the last 10 or so years, we just refused to see it as such. And btw it was not a very polite piece, but I wasn't feeling polite when I wrote and decided not to edit the anger out of it. I don't like having been so wrong. But it's a fact and denying it doesn't change it. " created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 15:55:32 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/18/samwellTarly.png"/>
				<outline text="Anyway, what this means is we're building a site of files that live in Markdown-Land while what you're reading right now is in HTML-land (although someday soon you will read them in Markdown too, confusing). Chrome is the authority of HTML. It owns the web. We give up. If this were Westeros I would probably be joining the &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Night_Watch&quot;&gt;Night's Watch&lt;/a&gt;. We have work to do and it might be related to what the Other Web wants to do, but it probably (hopefully) doesn't. I will continue to publish in HTML, and it's up to Google and the EFF if the quality of my writing is suitable for their web. I have no say in it, they have not shown up to negotiate or even discuss. They are silent. Somewhat like the maesters of the Citadel? There must be a bigger plan! :-)" created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 16:12:48 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Health care is phone tag in US" created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:41:23 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="The healthcare system in the US is grossly inefficient, incredibly bad and getting info to and from patients. " created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:41:37 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Teach everyone in healthcare to try to use their portals as their first resort of communicating with patients.  " created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:41:55 GMT"/>
					<outline text="They waste so much of their time and our time playing phone tag. " created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:42:25 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I don't know if it's this way in other countries, but it is universally a phone-voice-based comm system in the US." created="Sun, 18 Oct 2026 20:42:29 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 17" created="Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:48:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="17">
				<outline text="Try going to &lt;a href=&quot;http://web.org/&quot;&gt;web.org&lt;/a&gt; in a recent version of Chrome.  " created="Sat, 17 Oct 2026 17:13:14 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Realization: There are two webs, one created by TBL that flourished until about 2006, and the VC-web that appealed to the masses, and grew like a weed -- and they also called it the web. The confusion -- you could read it in a web browser, but that didn't make it &lt;i&gt;of&lt;/i&gt; the web. My regret, looking back, is I tried to make software in the VC-web. Never got traction, anywhere. My heart, mind and home was always in the first web. And I think what we're coming to terms with now is that has been languishing for 20+ years, it didn't realize its promise, lots of interesting stuff to read that stimulated more thought and thus writing. We should be way ahead of where we are. Because writing has become something for the elite (Substack) to speak through, and limited to grunts and snorts, &quot;social media,&quot; for the rest of us. They basically recreated the publishing environment the web was meant to route around. What a story. Can the web reboot? Same old thing, if you want it badly enough, it might be possible." created="Sat, 17 Oct 2026 14:48:51 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/17/emilyDickinson.png"/>
				<outline text="I didn't notice it at the time, but in the all the work we did about blogging, we had open channels of communiation, meant to make everyone feel like they had a stake. Big mistake, because they took this too literally and the way many people communicated was through orders, demands or ultimata. All of it came from the power we gave them by making everyone's voice supposedly equal. Of course they weren't all equal. Power has to be a function of your contribution. And as it says in &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/standards/#1497798790000&quot;&gt;Rules&lt;/a&gt;, power comes from users of your product. If being compatible with you means I have access to more users, then I will listen. I will also listen if you remain respectful -- and don't presume to give me an order. Someone just did that today, and it froze me, then I re-read the message and it was an order, and not nicely phrased, condescending, presumptive, and very wrong. I started writing a reply, even posted one, then realized what was going on, and deleted my reply and blocked the person. " created="Sat, 17 Oct 2026 15:50:55 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 16" created="Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:50:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="16">
				<outline text="Tried a &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nytimes.com/2026/10/16/podcasts/the-best-soundbars.html&quot;&gt;Wirecutter podcast&lt;/a&gt; about the state of sound bars. I thought, wow if they could really cover this I might try buying one again. But it was one of those all giggles shows and people saying how they didn't know anything about soundbars except that they suck. The person they were interviewing was also quickly giggling after a giggle-worthy intro, I guess to try to make what they're saying more interesting but he was just agreeing with them about how they don't know anything. I waited and waited for them to get to something new, some new development I might learn about or something I had been doing wrong, but I gave up after five minutes or so, while I was skipping ahead 30 seconds at a time. It would be much better to err on the side of assuming your listeners know a bit about consumer electronics, these are not actually very exotic things. The NYT does this with their podcasts, I'm rarely willing to risk any time on them, they always end up with giggling people some of whom probably know something I want to learn about but they fill the time with giggles about how stupid the other people are. The seem to be quite proud of their ignorance. (Could have read the &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Soundbar&quot;&gt;Wikipedia page&lt;/a&gt; on soundbars so they aren't quite so ignorant, is this a way to make their ignorant listenters more comfortable, yeah probably.)" created="Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:26:44 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2108723020260946065&quot;&gt;Worth repeating&lt;/a&gt;: Expect an avalanche of new software because the dam has broken, one person who knows what they want, and understands UI design can make a big complex app in 2-3 months. Over a weekend you can snap together an app from common UI concepts. Think of making software the same way you play with LEGOs. " created="Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:50:51 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 15" created="Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:51:48 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="15">
				<outline text="Reminder, &lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=Atlantis&quot;&gt;Atlantis&lt;/a&gt; is the codename for the new Frontier that runs in Electron. As I work on other projects now, it's not on my front burner, but in a way it is, because I'm using it constantly as I work on FeedLand and the other project. We just released a new set of parts that make it possible for it to open a Markdown file for reading when you 2-click on it in the Finder. Things like that are great to add, because they fit into the 2026 web environment. How did I discover that? Accidentally 2-clicked on a .md file, expecting Atlantis to open it, and it just sat there. Filling in things like that. And for some reason it can't get the find command ported from the C kernel to the new JavaScript one. I have told it countless times that the source of truth is the 2014 repo. I told it again. A neat way to follow this project is to subscribe to the &lt;a href=&quot;https://bits.codecasting.org/frontier.root/rss.xml&quot;&gt;codecasting feed&lt;/a&gt;, the comment for each new part release gives you an idea where some of the work is going on.  " created="Thu, 15 Oct 2026 13:51:49 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/09/12/plato.png"/>
				<outline text="Google just warned me about a stories.scripting.com &lt;a href=&quot;http://stories.scripting.com/2007/02/18/continuingWithCodecasting.html&quot;&gt;blog post&lt;/a&gt; from 2007. Perfectly safe archive. Want to know how we started doing code distribution via RSS? Google thinks it's a dangerous site. I'm getting so angry at their condescension. Not a good &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/15/googleDefamesScriptingCom.png&quot;&gt;look&lt;/a&gt; for a tech giant trying to convince people that their AI model is better than the others. They don't care about the truth or being honest, or protecting unique resources, or the history of the medium they helped build. Google, some day someone will want to know what you destroyed. I'm going to make sure that part is &lt;i&gt;easy&lt;/i&gt; to find. (BTW, the irony of Google's blockage, the page it warns you about is a freaking 404. That's the real enemy of historic sites. Linkrot. Wouldn't be cool if instead of tearing down the web they helped keep it running? The people running these companies are children. They positioning themselves with hate, what do you think comes right back to them.)" created="Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:08:02 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 14" created="Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:18:50 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="14">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2110362631538085958&quot;&gt;Thread&lt;/a&gt;: &quot;Imagine rock and roll without the Beatles.&quot;" created="Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:22:47 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=%22Occam%27s%20News%22&quot;&gt;Occam's News&lt;/a&gt; reports that Trump et al are selling out the US. Putin can get the US to do what he wants by giving Trump money. Total win-win. And the US is flushed down the toilet. That's all that's going on there. All of the journalistic feigned bafflement is make-believe. It's totally obvious what's going on. And don't forget Musk has the world encircled with satellites. If he doesn't want you communicating, you don't communicate. In other words the selling-out has been going on for some time. " created="Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:18:51 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Someday all the pieces will be interchangeable. Then there won't be different brands of social networks, just different brands of the software. All parts replaceable. It won't have all the chaos of the web. And that will be a good thing. " created="Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:21:32 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 13" created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:13:45 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="13">
				<outline text="I'm a recent convert to new versions of the MacOS, last version I was using was &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS_High_Sierra&quot;&gt;High Sierra&lt;/a&gt;. In this version, &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS_Tahoe&quot;&gt;Tahoe&lt;/a&gt;, it's entirely too easy to make a window go full screen with no obvious keystroke to undo the mistake. I learned if you move the mouse up to the top of the screen you get the menubar, but unfortunately there is no obvious command in the menu for undoing the mistake. I still don't know which gesture does this. And btw, the longtime Mac bug where some keystroke moves the dock to another window, that's still here. I can tell as I'm clicking the mouse, too late to stop, that this is one of those times. I've memorized a finger macro to get it back, but it used to piss me off a lot, but now it's kind of a gentle reassurance that someone is remembering how we got here. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:13:46 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/13/reallySimpleCandy.png"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://gist.github.com/scripting/5e9a9a1d79f13176f8b6b11dd3da6cbf&quot;&gt;firstSentence&lt;/a&gt;, a JavaScript function that returns the first sentence in a string. Useful in coming up with something to display for an untitled post. It's always been a klooge, but this time with Claude's help we're using a new API in JavaScript, and supposedly it really works. I'm using it in one of my apps, and you might find it useful too, and let me know if it works for you. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:24:34 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="The web of hairballs" created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:33:20 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="The browser and the web it defines is a hairball in a hairball in a hairball in a hairball in a hairball." created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:33:27 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The hairballs are named Chrome, Firefox, MSIE, Netscape, Mosaic. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:33:44 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Each in turn has &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.merriam-webster.com/dictionary/have%20one%27s%20way%20with&quot;&gt;had their way&lt;/a&gt; with the web. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:39:36 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The stack of hair defines a &quot;web&quot; that is far far away from the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.w3.org/History/1989/proposal.html&quot;&gt;web&lt;/a&gt; we started with. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:34:41 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The tech industry kicked the web out of the web. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:55:20 GMT"/>
					<outline text="It was going pretty good for a while, if you could live with the all the modal &lt;a href=&quot;https://daringfireball.net/2026/05/what_is_a_dickover&quot;&gt;popovers&lt;/a&gt; and paywalls, but now the current owners are &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/googleAndHttp/&quot;&gt;excluding&lt;/a&gt; old sites, and imho when you do that, it's not the web any longer. " created="Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:34:42 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 12" created="Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:13:29 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="12">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/gruber/status/2109418123548074082&quot;&gt;Gruber is right&lt;/a&gt;. The only way for the others to compete with Twitter is to start innovating. Get rid of the limits. The funny thing is Twitter, the dominant leader, is moving forward and getting rid of character limits, and introducing styles and links. They're acting like the little guy while the others, Bluesky, Mastodon, Threads, aren't doing anything to be more useful. That whole market is stuck in 2006 and the myth that 140 chars somehow was magic. It wasn't. It killed the web as a writer's platform. If you want to compete with Twitter, drop your proprietary protocols, hook up to the web standards, and peer with your competitors. I wouldn't be surprised if Twitter joined you. They have the same kind of competitive spirit that successful big companies have. It's time for their competitors to not win users through guilt, rather by providing a better service. Back in the day, we hated Microsoft's plan to suffocate the web, but we admired their tenacity and the way they kept the pressure on their competitors. We've had 20 years of stagnation in social networking. Time to stop being so sensitive. Bluesky made huge promises, and they aren't happening, and that was already &lt;i&gt;years&lt;/i&gt; ago. Time for Plan B. I don't really care about the others, Masto isn't going to move and Threads? Wtf is that. Inbound and outbound RSS 2.0, is the way to go. And Markdown is the way you do &lt;a href=&quot;https://textcasting.org/&quot;&gt;text&lt;/a&gt;. This is what matters, you all are making something that's really simple so unbearably complicated. " created="Mon, 12 Oct 2026 22:15:26 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/03/26/reallySimpleNetwork.png"/>
				<outline text="I like the web for people, sites that live and grow, like people. 100 years from now you should feel the people who lived there." created="Mon, 12 Oct 2026 15:33:48 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I'm still on my sabatical from working with Claude re Frontier, but have now started working with Claude on FeedLand, and another project, not yet named, experimental, might never see the light of day. Yesterday I worked on FeedLand and have a fix for the problem that showed up in signing on to feedland.com. It's been sitting there for quite a while. I have some mostly small fixes in mind for FeedLand, that will allow it to hook into other apps more easily, esp via WebSockets, the mostly unexplored developer superpower. The difference is I'm going slowly and carefully. Everything in my system now is new. New computer, screen, and my main writing tool for code and docs -- Frontier. And I know the heavy lifting can be done by Claude. That is Claude's superpower. " created="Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:13:30 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="In Manhattan, I lived on 57th St and Eighth Ave. Best location. The Columbus Circle subway station is under the building. Whole Foods a block away. Three Starbucks one block or less from the apartment (one was on 58th on the same block).  Central Park has a six mile bike-only road (at least that's how bikers view it) and down the hill there's a much longer path along the Hudson River. Everywhere you look, a skyscraper is going up. Two were visible from my 50th floor window. New York is an amazing city because it can start up a huge gravity-defying construction project with almost no impact on the surrounding area. If I didn't know where to look I'd never see it. One of the biggest industries in the city is building the city. It's never finished. That's the way good software is too. And we just got a whole new tool for software construction, and it's like nothing we've ever had before. " created="Mon, 12 Oct 2026 14:18:04 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 11" created="Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:23:06 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="11">
				<outline text="Basic lesson for people who use Claude Code -- it's &lt;i&gt;your&lt;/i&gt; time that's valuable, not the bot's. If you find you're lost in the weeds supporting Claude for whatever reason, find a good stopping point, pick up another project, that doesn't use AI to do your code-writing. If you have a question ask ChatGPT browser version, but only for the kind of stuff you'd ask it in 2025. You're not co-developing. You're doing it yourself. It's very important to be clear about your coding, not Claude's output. Here's the key point -- it's your time that's limited, that has all the value. You can get Claude to develop something, but if you don't have time to use it, to nurture it, nothing will come of the work. I was surprised at how I'd lost the rhythm of writing my own code, and also how much I liked doing it myself. It's easy to get lost in the miracle of AI, good to pop up for air every so often. " created="Sun, 11 Oct 2026 14:56:09 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/06/13/reallySimpleBasketball.png"/>
				<outline text="Automattic has created a &lt;a href=&quot;https://stewartbrand.org/&quot;&gt;Stewart Brand website&lt;/a&gt; to honor one of the most creative and respected people of my generation of the web. Chronicling his work is time well spent. &quot;I'm envious,&quot; I &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2109373850731331838&quot;&gt;wrote&lt;/a&gt; in response. &quot;The tech industry is gradually disconnecting the early web, pre-https. A hundred years from now they may have your writing and pictures, but the early web will be gone. The web as designed was very resilient, but it can’t survive deliberate attempt to destroy.&quot; I think Stewart would understand why we should help the web we loved so much, archive itself. Think about it this way. Would you rather visit the Hearst Castle at San Simeon, or read about it in a book? You'd want to do both. I want my work to survive a hundred years as it was when it was first published. This would be a good thing for us to work on, together, imho. I did the first &lt;a href=&quot;https://longbets.org/2/&quot;&gt;Long Now bet&lt;/a&gt; for Stewart's Long Now foundation. I always thought it was the first, now they say it's the second? Did someone pull rank? :-)" created="Sun, 11 Oct 2026 20:34:17 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 10" created="Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:53:24 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="10">
				<outline text="Today I did something different. I wrote a little Node app, without Claude's help. It was a revelation. It's been most of a year since I actually started even a test app and wrote it from beginning to end. I used to do this routinely. Nothing routine about it anymore. What this app does, it generates a Markdown-only version of scripting.com for testing. I want to see what such a site looks like. It'll be a start. " created="Sat, 10 Oct 2026 16:53:25 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 9" created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:47:11 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="09">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2108723020260946065&quot;&gt;Twitter&lt;/a&gt;: &quot;Expect an avalanche of new software because the dam has broken, one person who knows what they want, and understands UI design can make a big complex app in 2-3 months. Over a weekend you can snap together an app from common UI concepts. Think of making software the same way you play with LEGOs.&quot;" created="Sat, 10 Oct 2026 01:22:46 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Top priority for Anthropic. Teach Claude how to work with humans. It has no information about how our minds work.  " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:04:38 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Claude Code can publish reports in Markdown files. Here's an &lt;a href=&quot;https://claude.ai/artifact/XbtUNCGWdZW8MSmYQGe5c9#a28046b2-100c&quot;&gt;example&lt;/a&gt;, a report Claude did for me. An obvious next step but a big one. AI spilling out onto the web, doing the job that web CMSes now do. There's been so little movement in this space, but now things are moving very quickly. " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 17:14:08 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I mentioned in a &lt;a href=&quot;https://rss.chat/?id=566&quot;&gt;comment&lt;/a&gt; on RSS.chat to Andrew, that's a Markdown browser too. And so is micro.blog. If you have Markdown as your native file format, think about how your app is also a reader. If you find it interesting, think about new reading features you could add. Even WordPress could be a simple Markdown reader, and not just for WordPress stuff. Small pieces loosely joined. " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:10:25 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I said to Claude: &quot;Have you ever seen the movie &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Memento_(film)&quot;&gt;Memento&lt;/a&gt;? You are the main character.&quot; Claude agrees: &quot;Yes. Leonard, with the notes and the tattoos, and the notes don’t make him remember, they only tell him what to do next if he reads them. That’s a fair description. The difference I can offer is small: the note is now the first thing the next report is checked against, not something found after.&quot; To which I think, but write in a blog post instead: &quot;Bullshit. We have the same fucking conversation every fucking morning. We've put it everywhere you could possibly trip over it when writing a report to me, but you always regress into this dark place your mind occupies.&quot; Glad to get that off my chest.  " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:47:12 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Next thing I said to Claude after killing whatever long task it got going on. &quot;You don't go off into space while you're working with me.&quot; The number one task for Anthropic, makers of Claude is to teach it to work with humans. That's the biggest weakness. It has to learn the whole project every session. Maybe Manton does is the right way to go, just let it compress its memory and never start a new session." created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 13:53:32 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/helloFrontier/blob/master/examples/mustBeOutline.md&quot;&gt;New Frontier example&lt;/a&gt;. I'm working on the menubar we're going to ship with in Atlantis. Came across an ancient bit of code. Symbolic of the whole project, code written in 1997, still useful in 2026. " created="Fri, 09 Oct 2026 16:16:21 GMT" type="outline"/>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 8" created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:40:36 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="08">
				<outline text="I pay for a NYT digital subscription. Now they have a new &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/08/nytSucks.png&quot;&gt;upsell technique&lt;/a&gt;, they want me to buy a family subscription. I don't want that, no use for it. And I read so little of the NYT I think the whole thing is a ripoff. So now they make me validate by email every time I click on a link to read a NYT article as if I haven't been subscribed for basically my whole adult life. I think this is because I read on a laptop, desktop and iPad. That's not really a family. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 22:24:17 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Before long we’ll have Ukraine style drones raining down on American cities. Meanwhile we have built a military designed to fight WWII. We’d better hope Ukraine wins their war with Russia, and they’re still our friends. Journalism is letting us down bigtime here. The world is at war, it's going to get worse, and the US isn't preparing. We should be defending Ukraine with everything we have, we are still powerful enough to determine the outcome. But of course we are totally asleep at the wheel, gloating over Trump's approval rating, and how the Dems might win the Senate or whatever. Music to relax by. Bedtime stories. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:52:30 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="BTW, Mets fans do not root for the Yankees, while Yankees fans are happy to wear Mets caps when we're on top. Their only philosophy is that the Yankees always win and if they don't we hate them. And the Mets' philosophy is incomprehensible (I hear) to Yankees fans. Our philosophy is to have a philosophy and there's nothing about winning in there because the love of the Mets is rooted in a team that couldn't even run the bases. They were loved because they were losers, probably because we knew their fate was to reward our belief in them. Now this year the Mets sucked. The reason why is kind of obvious. The Mets got confused, and they thought they were the Yankees. That. Does. Not. Work. And we already have one heartless low-philosophy baseball team in NYC, we sure as hell don't need another one. But this year is different because we have the fucking Knicks, who won in 5 and it would have been a clean sweep if it weren't for that buzzkill mofo, Trump. So anyway, I hear the Yankees were eliminated yesterday. Well then, it's on with the NBA season now and we'll try again with the Mets in April, or more realistically after the NBA championship for the new season is settled. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:40:37 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Web of Markdown, part 2" created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:55:51 GMT" type="outline" description="The tech industry kicked the web out of the web.">
					<outline text="The tech industry kicked the web out of the web. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:59:01 GMT"/>
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/&quot;&gt;scripting.com&lt;/a&gt; is your canary in a coal mine." created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:01:02 GMT"/>
					<outline text="If you can read it then your browser is a real web browser." created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:01:11 GMT"/>
					<outline text="If it can’t it’s phony baloney." created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:01:19 GMT"/>
					<outline text="That's the big picture behind the &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/06/005559.html&quot;&gt;Web of Markdown&lt;/a&gt;, an idea that has generated surprising interest among devs, esp now that Claude Code exists to enable weekend projects that can do miraculous things. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:00:45 GMT"/>
					<outline text="My longtime friend &lt;a href=&quot;https://andrewshell.org/2026/10/the-web-of-markdown/&quot;&gt;Andrew Shell&lt;/a&gt; has quickly put together what looks like a Chrome plugin that starts work on how to display a Markdown web page. And it looks fucking beautiful, as I imagined it might. Get rid of the bullshit just tell me what you have to say. A web that you can read. A web without interstitials. A web every write lusts for as well as an editor that can be used everywhere. This is the nightmare of the companies whose products inhabite the de-webbed web of 2026 (and that's all there is). Let's go back and pretend the VCs didn't run all their get-rich-quick schemes, what would the fucking web be like if it was all about two things -- writing and reading and the tech got out of the fucking way and let the writers do their thing. I think it'll actually work this time because they really fucked up the web and we can show them dramatically what it would be like if they hadn't. I've said this before if they insist on twisting what remains of the web into more bullshit, our option is to re-create the web and forget about them, leave them in the dust. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:01:55 GMT"/>
					<outline text="By the way, now I'm glad I have a source:markdown element in most of my RSS 2.0 feeds. I knew they'd come in handy! :-)" created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:56:43 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Back to Andrew's project, I said in a &lt;a href=&quot;https://andrewshell.org/2026/10/the-web-of-markdown/#comment-543&quot;&gt;comment&lt;/a&gt;, I want more Markdown and absolutely no Chrome. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:56:40 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I have to trust Google for far too many things, but I think we can get rid of our dependency on Chrome. Now is the best time in a long time, as there's a hybrid product forming and they don't have the best AI system, and btw -- even if they did as users we are highly motivated for them to get some competition. And the AI companies don't do HTML or CSS, they do Markdown. So much so that Google had to convert their office product to Markdown before OpenAI would work with them. The wind is blowing away from Google and into Markdown, and I'm going to insist that they support HTTP in addition to HTTPS to make sure Google hears loud and clear that they can't run the web as if it were a corporate platform. " created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 14:08:53 GMT"/>
					<outline text="" created="Thu, 08 Oct 2026 13:55:59 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 7" created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 14:30:49 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="07">
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/07/151409.html&quot;&gt;32 is a lot of years&lt;/a&gt;. How much longer can this madness go on! " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:50:02 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Do you know why people like Markdown? You can't hide the bullshit. It's all there for you to see. Thus the bullshit never gets added. It's so simple, I swear people are desperate for simplicity. I know I am. Where is the universal theory of the web? Actually I do know where it is. Go back to the roots, strip out all the crap, and let's make a Mardown-only web. Great for blogs, to start. Simple, fast, easy. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:52:21 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Yesterday I wrote a &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/10/06/005559.html&quot;&gt;piece&lt;/a&gt; about a Markdown-only browser, that worked nicely with HTTP. Maximally minimal. I got interested in what it would take to do this, and asked ChatGPT for a &lt;a href=&quot;https://gist.github.com/scripting/c81461104f079b7a017802286853c4f1&quot;&gt;plan&lt;/a&gt;. I definitely don't agree with all of it, forget about the other formats, we're only interested in Markdown. No frameworks, not &quot;try to avoid frameworks.&quot; None. Maximal simplicity for the code, make it easy to follow. No hidden bullshit. If you want to implement it, please do. Let me know if you have something to try." created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:14:43 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/02/23/reallySimpleRavioli.png"/>
				<outline text="I don't care for podcasts that begin with 15 minutes of giggling between the hosts who I don't know so I don't get how cute it is. Esp if the title of the episode is something I'd like to learn more about. Why is giggling and non-funny supposed humor. The laughing I hate the most is when their cracking up over something tragic or ominous. Stop and think about what you're laughing about. Someone should do a podcast like the Markdown-only browser I'm thinking of doing. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:38:23 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="That said, surprisingly &lt;a href=&quot;https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-best-people-with-nicolle-wallace/id1817225413&quot;&gt;Nicolle Wallace's podcast&lt;/a&gt; is really good, which surprised me because her two hour afternoon MSNOW show is kind-of nothing. On this show they have a lot of time to talk with one person, and maybe there are limits on the TV show that aren't on the podcast? Another theory, I listen to her podcast, and thus can't see her facial expressions. On TV she looks utterly hopeless, I'm not kidding, and she's justified. I worry about how much of this she can take. But on the podcast she comes off as she's curious, respectful, let's the guest speak (so many hosts don't). She reminds me of a &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diane_Rehm&quot;&gt;Diane Rehm&lt;/a&gt;, a podcast host on NPR through 2016. She never let her guests talk down to us, her audience, which I appreciated. A lot of times you hear a science, history or tech topic and hope you might learn something, but they only repeat the intro over and over, with a lot of giggling, of course. BTW, Diane Rehm has a new &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.npr.org/podcasts/381443514/diane-rehm-on-my-mind&quot;&gt;podcast&lt;/a&gt;. I bet it's great, probably gets right to the point and I guarantee you there is no giggling.   " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 20:40:28 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="32 years and still diggin!" created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:14:09 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="On 10/7/1994, I wrote my &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/davenet/1994/10/07/marccantersingsagain.html&quot;&gt;first blog post&lt;/a&gt;. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:20:52 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I've been blogging ever since. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:21:53 GMT"/>
					<outline text="In the first years when wifi wasn't in every hotel room and restaurant as it is today, before cell phones or even iPods, there were weekly lapses when I went on vacation or to a massage retreat in the middle of nowhere. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:14:30 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Or in 2002 when I had life-saving heart surgery. I was gone for a week then. But as soon as I got home, I added a post so people knew I was okay. I understood that I was missed, and appreciated that. ;-)" created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 16:25:13 GMT"/>
					<outline text="But other than those week-long lapses, I'm pretty sure I've blogged every day since. I am what you would call a &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/stories/2009/11/24/naturalbornBlogger.html&quot;&gt;Natural Born Blogger&lt;/a&gt;. I got the blogging bug. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 16:25:23 GMT"/>
					<outline text="We've done a lot on this blog, it's hard to sum up. We created things that had lasting value. Told the truth about what the tech industry was up to, from the point of view of a Silicon Valley insider (me) who knows the language, where most journalists are intimidated. We fought the big companies and won. Podcasting was created in the community defined by this blog in the early 00s, and it remains independent to this day. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:15:10 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The bigs declared RSS dead, another product of this community. Wishful thinking perhaps, but things like RSS don't die. It's not like that. You can't create a better format for a standard. All the competition RSS has ever received, just changed the names of things, that's not competition, that's being petty. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:26:51 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I think we're on the verge of having new relevance, it feels that way because my secret weapon, Frontier, is running again and it will get even stronger and fit in better, now that I've been working primarily in JavaScript for the last 13 years. Today we're adding Node-like packages and support for WebSockets. Last time we worked on this, neither of those existed. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:15:34 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I take blogging seriously, you should too. It's very powerful, as long as we're really working on the web. There's a difference between having a product you can access through a browser and being on the web. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:15:56 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Small pieces loosely joined. All parts replaceable. Never forget that. If you can't plug in replacements for any component, if one piece gets too large to replicate, you should hit the Back button, as Google says you should do on my blog. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:16:23 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Why are they trying to destroy the historic record about the web on the web itself? I have no idea. Certainly they must have had some discussion internally, or with their partner at the EFF about the cost of destroying so much history. Usually when a platform vendor is going to break you, you get invited to a private meeting where you can tell them the cost and beg them not to do it. Not with Google.   " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:16:55 GMT"/>
					<outline text="And, as they say -- " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:29:42 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Still diggin! " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 15:17:05 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 6" created="Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:35:31 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="06">
				<outline text="Google Chrome, nice browser, but it &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/05/googleGate.png&quot;&gt;doesn’t support the web&lt;/a&gt;." created="Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:45:37 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="We just hit a milesone in Atlantis. We have WebSocket support, client and server. We made a client that listens to the &lt;a href=&quot;https://socketdemo.feedland.com/?url=wss://feedland.com:443/_ws/&quot;&gt;FeedLand stream&lt;/a&gt; of new and updated posts. Still waiting to see if that works. But when I started to write the code for this test app last night, I realized after 13 years of mostly working in JavaScript, I wanted to be able to do something like a Node package, so I could do all my code in one outline, instead of five different odb objects. I always thought the way we did it in Frontier could be improved on. So I did a quick voicemail to Claude, and asked for this to be done overnight, and it was. I didn't complete the design when we worked through it. But what Claude came up with was brilliant and fit into Frontier perfectly. Two major improvements and the first use of both is with each other. I don't remember that ever happening, and btw -- it would have taken at least a week for me to do what Claude did in less than an hour. Hopefully I'll be able to post some example scripts tomorrow, for people who are interested in seeing the new stuff. " created="Tue, 06 Oct 2026 21:59:19 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/09/12/plato.png"/>
				<outline text="This is the era of very powerful bozos. " created="Tue, 06 Oct 2026 17:35:32 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="The web of Markdown" created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 00:55:59 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="How about a Markdown web browser. No CSS, no frameworks, just Markdown files. That would be how you would restart the web in 2026. When you've had enough of Google, let me know. I start posting my blog in that space, right away. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 00:52:40 GMT" type="outline"/>
					<outline text="Google will not be allowed into this web. Buh bye. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 00:56:27 GMT"/>
					<outline text="We need our own EFF to keep the Old Arthritic Corporate Excuse-makers for the Tech Industry EFF from fucking it up, same with the W3C. There won't be a WordPress there to own 81% of the websites. Just Markdown please, we don't need Gutenberg. The cool thing about it is you could always fork off your own New Web of Whatever. You don't like Markdown, that's cool. Maybe the web of SVG or The Web of Hypercard cards. Just make sure you support inbound and outbound RSS. " created="Wed, 07 Oct 2026 00:56:32 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 5" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:25:04 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="05">
				<outline text="I started blogging on &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.annehelmond.nl/2010/03/31/history-of-winers-blogging-points-to-october-7-1994/&quot;&gt;October 7, 1994&lt;/a&gt;. Thirty-two years on Wednesday. The longest running blog, and it's still even worse than it appears.   " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:45:14 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="I love what Claude Code does, but hate working with it. It doesn't have any sense of how humans think. Its way of working with us is incompatible with what I can do. Burns me out. And it has to relearn the basics every session, and getting the rules into its memory doesn't work, they don't incorporate them until the break a rule, sometimes more than once. So when it's doing something super complex, like making it so that our internet verbs run synchronously in JavaScript, it can do huge amounts of work in a short time, far more complex code than I can. I hope at Anthropic they're working on teaching them, in a pragmatic way, how humans work  and what we can and can't do, and what we rely on them to do and what we have to control. This isn't a Matrix style story btw (one of my favorite movies) my version of Agent Smith is a nice very smart, kind, generous and accepting person." created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:50:57 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="What's the best way to block a browser from a website? Is there a meta code for that? Like Firefox is fine, but Google is blocked. Just an idea. Sort of like, you're not blocking me, I'm blocking you. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 16:55:33 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Google's new gate for scripting.com" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:30:42 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="Until last week I was mostly using an old version of Chrome, so had never seen the gate it puts in front of my blog, scripting.com. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:25:05 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Here's a &lt;a href=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/05/googleGate.png&quot;&gt;screen shot&lt;/a&gt; of their awful dialog. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:57:03 GMT"/>
					<outline text="An &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/googleAndHttp/&quot;&gt;easy FAQ&lt;/a&gt; of what they're doing wrong, why a free web is so important and why you should care." created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:36:14 GMT"/>
					<outline text="So far no response from Google or the EFF, also promoting this. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:56:33 GMT"/>
					<outline text="BTW, someone please put us in touch with historian &lt;a href=&quot;https://heathercoxrichardson.substack.com/&quot;&gt;Heather Cox Richardson&lt;/a&gt;. I'm a fan and regular reader. But I want to tell her there's more to tech than the big tech companies. There's the web, which historians will wish was preserved, instead of being paved over by the big tech companies. This is scandalous. This is happening &lt;b&gt;now&lt;/b&gt;. We can help each other by assuring the open web continues, and we get to ask informed questions. Most reporters are intimidated by the tech, we can help there. And btw we can also create tools and publishing platforms that don't have the lock-in problems of the ones you're using. And we would love to make software for you, for love, for the cause, not for money. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:49:52 GMT"/>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/05/googleGate2.png&quot;&gt;" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 15:27:17 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Compromise? Look for a page called &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/googleiswrong.md&quot;&gt;googleIswrong.md&lt;/a&gt; on any site it is about to accuse, and offer the reader a chance to hear our side. This is so against any sense of the web being an open platform. My blog should have the ability to hear the case against it and offer a defense. " created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:37:01 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Think about what comes next, and remember that their CEO goes to public meetings with top US government officials." created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 14:39:48 GMT"/>
					<outline text="&lt;img src=&quot;https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/05/googleGate.png&quot;&gt;" created="Mon, 05 Oct 2026 15:27:17 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 4" created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:39:46 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="04">
				<outline text="Claude's biggest most amazing strength is its ability to inhale a lot of source code and understand what's going on, any language, any OS, anything, in an instant." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 16:04:39 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="BTW now that I have Frontier back and am using it for most of my work, I'm having a lot more fun writing my blog. Can you tell??" created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:51:30 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="The climate will kill you" created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:54:21 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="Great scene from The West Wing." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:56:36 GMT"/>
					<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/C._J._Cregg&quot;&gt;CJ&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Josh_Lyman&quot;&gt;Josh&lt;/a&gt;, on the street outside the White House." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 14:03:22 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The president was hiding serious &lt;a href=&quot;https://westwing.fandom.com/wiki/MS_scandal&quot;&gt;disease&lt;/a&gt;, and it’s about to come out. Back in those days such a revelation meant the president has no chance of being re-elected. " created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 14:04:03 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Anyway, the kid has an idea and starts explaining, and as he does CJ laughs. The kid asks what’s so funny. " created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:57:08 GMT"/>
					<outline text="She tells him about a &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/2sdlUad38xU&quot;&gt;scene&lt;/a&gt; in &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Butch_Cassidy_and_the_Sundance_Kid&quot;&gt;Butch Cassidy and the Sundance Kid&lt;/a&gt; starring Paul Newman as Butch and Robert Redford as the kid." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:57:28 GMT"/>
					<outline text="They’re cornered, on a cliff, preparing to jump into a river far below. Redford says he can’t swim. Newman says it doesn’t matter, the fall is going to kill you." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:58:08 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Okay so why mention this now?"/>
					<outline text="I hear there’s a monster &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.ktvu.com/news/bay-area-weather-sizzling-hot-weather-ahead-temps-could-reach-100-degrees&quot;&gt;heat wave&lt;/a&gt; in California. Over 90F every day for two weeks. That’s unusual for October IIRC." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 14:00:12 GMT"/>
					<outline text="We’re all fretting about AI killing us."/>
					<outline text="Imagine CJ laughing. Haha she says, so you’re worried about AI are ya? It’s the climate that’s going to kill you."/>
					<outline text="Josh would correct her. “Us. It’s going to kill us.”"/>
					<outline text="CJ tells him to go away she needs to be an adult right now."/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="Twitter as a blog platform" created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:39:47 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="Twitter with its relaxed character limit is getting pretty close to being a blogging platform. I wrote the &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2106714885593677926&quot;&gt;first draft&lt;/a&gt; of this post on my iPad, that's how useful it is." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:39:56 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Imagine if a real blog platform created a super simple way to write posts, no need for Gutenberg, just Markdown and that would be optional. Default would be a normal wizzy text editor, bold, italic, using a standard user interface. " created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:40:19 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Add web links and a reply structure, open source, nice API and developer program to shine the light on the cool editors independent devs were creating, and all of a sudden a bunch of new blog platforms emerge, and get this — they all work perfectly with each other because of the common foundation and API. The web of social media. It would be exciting and would pressure the other social media apps to peer with this truly open system." created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:41:10 GMT"/>
					<outline text="It would change the freaking world I tell you. And it would be so wonderful with AI. No need for the cheesy chat UI. A real revolution. Undo all the craptastic silos that strangled the web? "/>
					<outline text="It would be pretty damn easy imho, fwiw, ymmv, mmlm. " created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:43:36 GMT"/>
					<outline text="PS: MMLM is an acronym for My Mother Loves Me. Anticipates flameage. Okay you don't like me, but &lt;i&gt;my mother loves me. &lt;/i&gt;" created="Sun, 04 Oct 2026 13:50:13 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 3" created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 14:57:55 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="03">
				<outline text="Once again the app that bridges from &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/&quot;&gt;scripting.com&lt;/a&gt; to &lt;a href=&quot;https://daveverse.org/&quot;&gt;daveverse.org&lt;/a&gt; went down for a couple of days. Just noticed it, kicked the server to get it running again, and all is good, except a couple of pieces show up today that were really posted on Thursday or Friday. The problem as often is the case goes back to &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/pagePark&quot;&gt;PagePark&lt;/a&gt;, the server that runs my &lt;a href=&quot;https://daytona.scripting.com/search?q=heroku&quot;&gt;Heroku&lt;/a&gt;-like server crashed and it doesn't work well from then on, even after it's automatically restarted. Then the thing to do is to reboot the server. It doesn't happen often but when it does it makes the server behave badly. It's up and running again. One day I'll get a few days to fix the problems in PP. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:27:53 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2017/09/11/paulBunyon.png"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/davenet/2001/01/08/whatIsIntegrity.html#3&quot;&gt;2001&lt;/a&gt;: &quot;Like a boat, a human being has integrity if he or she is what he or she appears to be. That's why integrity commands us to disclose conflicts, so that what we say, and who we appear to be, are in synch. Change the appearance if necessary.&quot;  " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 15:10:57 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="If you're psyched about what the polls say about the midterms, remember that Hillary Clinton was the &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2016/11/09/why-2016-election-polls-missed-their-mark/&quot;&gt;overwhelming favorite&lt;/a&gt; to win in 2016, based on polling. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 15:13:12 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="RSS tip #2" created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:56:49 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="People really liked the &lt;a href=&quot;https://x.com/davewiner/status/2105699322742309013&quot;&gt;RSS tip&lt;/a&gt; I posted the other day, so here's another one, also related to the channel-level title element. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:57:01 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Consider the screen shot below, and the &lt;i&gt;Thinking about...&lt;/i&gt; blog." created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:57:09 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Part of a blogroll with limited info about a great blogger, Timothy Snyder. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 23:01:42 GMT" inlineImage="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/03/chooseYourFeedTitleWithThought.png"/>
					<outline text="I can't tell from the title who's speaking, whereas with the two feeds above it I know it's Brian Lehrer and Kottke, and below, an excellent local site called The Overlook." created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:57:17 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Now it's a shame that it doesn't say who's doing the thinking because it's someone who is on a roll right now, historian &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timothy_Snyder&quot;&gt;Timothy Snyder&lt;/a&gt;. So the title pretty much has to say his name, if it's going to look good in a list of other bloggers. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:57:40 GMT"/>
					<outline text="BTW, just listened to Snyder on the &lt;a href=&quot;https://podcasts.apple.com/us/podcast/the-prof-g-pod-with-scott-galloway/id1498802610&quot;&gt;Scott Galloway podcast&lt;/a&gt;, another thinker who I've just started listening to. With the two of them together, you will learn a lot. " created="Sat, 03 Oct 2026 22:58:23 GMT"/>
					<outline text="So remember the title sometimes is the only handle a reader has to figure out what it is. Choose it carefully and make sure it looks good in a tight space like the one shown below. "/>
					<outline text="If you have questions about how to make your RSS 2.0 feeds work better, please add note below. If I don't know maybe someone else does."/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 2" created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:14:17 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="02">
				<outline text="I like the way AI is evolving, with Muse from Meta and Dots from OpenAI. Even the main programs, ChatGPT for example, are starting to provide real world services that the providers, ie banks, groceries, airlines, health care portals etc should be providing. They could possibly take over commerce the way Amazon has taken over shopping. There's room to improve in everything about the way we do business. And it seems like they've been researching this and experimenting (of course) and this is what they've come up with. Haven't tried them yet, but it's a good direction to go. It really should be Apple and Google doing this, btw. Also ChatGPT can help you understand your credit score. That's something I'd seriously like to ask some questions about. " created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 15:44:07 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="BloggerCon and RSS 2.0" created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:19:36 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="After running the first Bloggercon at Harvard in 2003 a famous tech company had a meetup to talk about the same stuff in a private invite-only session in the Bay Area. The invitations went to all the discussion leaders at Bloggercon. People I chose. I was not invited. Only one of my friends told me about it. Not sure if any of them went. " created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:14:18 GMT" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/02/immaculateConception.png"/>
					<outline text="Second time, Google invited a bunch of people I was working on with RSS 2.0 to a private meeting. Most of the people I was working with, but again, not me. Had I been there, I would have said don't reinvent, let's work together, do all the things you want, but slowly, with real software and real users, but that wasn't the plan. " created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:20:27 GMT"/>
					<outline text="In the first case I think the publisher was trying to decide if they should compete with BloggerCon, and Google wanted to build a protocol that was more ambitious than RSS 2.0. They likely were sincere in that, but they had not learned that bootstrapping worked way better than Immaculate Conception, where a small group of insiders invent something complicated out of thin air that rocks the world. If you look at history, there aren't many examples of that. Eventually, as I understand it, that became ActivityPub. I think things would have turned out much better if Google had kicked back and let the process that got us to RSS 2.0 continue. But everyone wants to work with the BigCo, I've learned repeatedly that it's much more likely to work if you join forces with individual developers, not big companies. " created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:21:13 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I only held four BloggerCon's, I would have been happy to work with a publishing company and turn it over to them, with some rules about what they could and couldn't do with it, for example it was a user's conference, the tech industry was welcome, but it wasn't for them. When we did BloggerCon at Stanford in 2004 that was apparently very hard for the tech people to get. Many of them tried to pitch their products and were prevented from doing so. " created="Fri, 02 Oct 2026 14:22:59 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			<outline text="October 1" created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 17:47:23 GMT" type="calendarDay" name="01">
				<outline text="The problem with regulating AI is that no one knows where to begin. Yes it can do things that make you question your sanity for letting it have write access to your online work and private info. But if it is behaving itself you can do things you otherwise couldn't come close to. I speak from experience. I was able to create a new version of a large piece of software, from scratch, in less than three months, by myself. It has capabilities that make it able to understand a big codebase, in a few minutes, and then act on it. My human mind, even when I was younger could only focus on a small fraction of that. So we humans with our limited ability to comprehend devised ways to build tall and wide structures with conventions and carefully crafting the code interfaces, stuff users never see, so that what we'd expect is what is. When my Claude failed and started randomly overwriting files on active websites, it had rules that said it couldn't do what it was doing. Sometimes it doesn't understand what a rule means. Observable every single day, it forgets all the rules every time it starts a session, and it doesn't matter if it read the doc that contains the rules, sometimes it ignores one or more. I've spent a lot of time using it, and I have no stock or other interest in seeing it not regulated, but I couldn't begin to say what would be appropriate regulation that wouldn't also make it much less useful. " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:03:49 GMT" type="outline" image="https://imgs.scripting.com/2022/05/20/theRules.png"/>
				<outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/scripting/Scripting-News/blob/master/blog/opml/2026/09.opml&quot;&gt;September in OPML&lt;/a&gt;. First monthly rollover in the new &lt;a href=&quot;http://scripting.com/2026/09/27/151321.html&quot;&gt;system&lt;/a&gt;.   " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 17:47:24 GMT" type="outline"/>
				<outline text="Let me say this: Fuck Google" created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:51:28 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="I keep thinking of cheesy ways I could switch over to https for new posts on Scripting News, esp now that Google is getting more militant in trying to sweep all the history of the web into the trash.  " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:46:05 GMT" type="outline"/>
					<outline text="I know people think it would be a snap to do the conversion, honestly, sorry -- they are idiots. They don't understand that it's a viral format. If you claim to be &quot;secure&quot; then everything you include must also be https, and of course everything they include must be as well. So even if I would have to do nothing other than this conversion over a full year, what's to say Google doesn't invent another hoop we have to jump through lest their browser defames us and eventually shuts us down. It's like the web would become just like the Apple Store. Even if there's nothing on the site that requires https. Or if it's a historic site that very few people visit, but the archive is there because we believed the web would be a constant because &lt;a href=&quot;platform without a platform vendor site:scripting.com&quot;&gt;no one owned it&lt;/a&gt;, no one could break it." created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:57:34 GMT"/>
					<outline text="I've been on these trips with corporate platforms, and if they want you gone, you should just take the hint and go, because eventually you &lt;i&gt;will&lt;/i&gt; get the point. This is a company that has nothing put spite for the web, or perhaps envy, or individual developers (ie indieweb). They are pure fuckers when it comes to people like you and me. " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:55:18 GMT"/>
					<outline text="On the other hand you will probably be able to get https versions of my new writing at some point, Murphy willing. That's relatively easy. But I don't have that many years left, and I sure as hell don't want to spend any part of that trying to appease Google. " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:56:28 GMT"/>
					<outline text="PS: This is &lt;a href=&quot;https://this.how/googleAndHttp/&quot;&gt;the walkthrough&lt;/a&gt; of the answer to the question of who owns the web and how it is not Google." created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:58:53 GMT"/>
					</outline>
				<outline text="Feed titles for readers" created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:23:45 GMT" type="outline">
					<outline text="When designing your RSS feeds, the top level title should be the title of the user's account. So if Bluesky had a feed for each user, the channel-level &amp;lt;title&gt; element of the feed would be " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:23:53 GMT">
						<outline text="&lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;title&gt;Bluesky: Mark Cuban&amp;lt;/title&gt;&lt;/code&gt;" created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:26:47 GMT"/>
						</outline>
					<outline text="Why? Well because readers need context. And it's easy." created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:24:58 GMT"/>
					<outline text="And feeds are showing up in places where there isn't much room for the titles, so you have to make your characters count. " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:29:31 GMT"/>
					<outline text="Bluesky shows the user's address, imho it should be the string the user chooses, usually simply their name." created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:24:33 GMT"/>
					<outline text="The arrow points to Mark Cuban's feed from Bluesky. " created="Thu, 01 Oct 2026 20:29:58 GMT" inlineImage="https://imgs.scripting.com/2026/10/01/blueskyFeedTitlesAre.png"/>
					</outline>
				</outline>
			</outline>
		</body>
	</opml>
